WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.325
A CI job runs your script on every push.

2
00:00:05.325 --> 00:00:08.409
It cannot read the messages your script prints.

3
00:00:08.633 --> 00:00:11.272
It looks at one number, the exit code.

4
00:00:11.272 --> 00:00:14.258
So do systemd, cron, and Kubernetes probes.

5
00:00:14.258 --> 00:00:18.147
They decide success or failure from the exit code alone.

6
00:00:18.867 --> 00:00:20.686
So picture the script as a box.

7
00:00:20.686 --> 00:00:24.149
Things come in on the left, and results leave on the right.

8
00:00:24.367 --> 00:00:29.472
Inputs come from three places: arguments, environment variables, and files.

9
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:35.659
And results leave on three separate channels: stdout, stderr, and the exit code.

10
00:00:35.867 --> 00:00:39.979
argparse turns the words of the command line into named values.

11
00:00:39.979 --> 00:00:42.003
Like the limit, max error rate.

12
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:45.909
The same setting can also come from an environment variable.

13
00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:47.426
So which one wins?

14
00:00:47.426 --> 00:00:51.303
argparse uses a default only when the flag is missing.

15
00:00:51.303 --> 00:00:56.329
So a flag beats the variable, and the variable beats the built-in 1.0.

16
00:00:57.033 --> 00:01:01.328
The file is the access log, with 238 requests in it.

17
00:01:01.533 --> 00:01:03.523
Run it with no limit given.

18
00:01:03.523 --> 00:01:05.365
The default is 1 percent.

19
00:01:05.567 --> 00:01:09.931
It counts 11 server errors out of 238 requests.

20
00:01:09.931 --> 00:01:11.788
That is 4.6 percent.

21
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:16.188
The report goes to stdout, the only stream a pipe passes on.

22
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.652
The reason it failed goes to stderr, in one line for people.

23
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:25.548
And 4.6 is above the 1 percent limit, so the exit code is 1.

24
00:01:25.548 --> 00:01:27.187
The CI job turns red.

25
00:01:27.187 --> 00:01:29.761
You wrote no extra code for that.

26
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:34.511
Raise the limit to 5 with the flag, and the same log passes.

27
00:01:34.511 --> 00:01:35.420
Exit code 0.

28
00:01:35.633 --> 00:01:41.863
With format json, stdout holds only JSON, because every message went to stderr.

29
00:01:41.863 --> 00:01:45.332
So you can pipe it into jq, with no cleanup.

30
00:01:46.033 --> 00:01:48.793
Every script in the lab keeps one contract.

31
00:01:48.793 --> 00:01:50.077
Zero means all good.

32
00:01:50.077 --> 00:01:52.388
One means the check found a problem.

33
00:01:52.388 --> 00:01:55.084
Two means the script could not do its job.

34
00:01:55.300 --> 00:01:57.483
Give it a file that is not there.

35
00:01:57.483 --> 00:02:02.972
The script catches FileNotFoundError by name, prints one clear line, and returns 2.

36
00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.305
Now a caller can tell the error rate is too high apart from the log file is missing.

37
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:10.814
Here is the mistake to avoid.

38
00:02:10.814 --> 00:02:13.761
Wrap the whole script in except Exception, pass.

39
00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:16.603
Now a crash becomes a silent success.

40
00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:20.807
The script exits 0, CI goes green, and nothing was checked.

41
00:02:21.033 --> 00:02:23.444
Catch the errors you expect, by name.

42
00:02:23.444 --> 00:02:30.220
Say on stderr what went wrong, return a non-zero code, and let everything else crash with its traceback.

43
00:02:30.933 --> 00:02:37.372
So: the report goes to stdout, the reasons go to stderr, and the verdict is the exit code.

44
00:02:37.372 --> 00:02:40.376
That number is the whole interface for CI.
